#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e142022-0a13-5572-a090-e4c3e3f9d6c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360044} {:answer/id #uuid "e093deb8-af07-57a4-a3c5-9d5cabe67cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360045} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee19c44-0e62-5f92-b07d-c2f384f63fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360046} {:answer/id #uuid "61dac524-823b-5e13-b56d-133c28aec53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360047} {:answer/id #uuid "2ac479b4-059e-536d-ab39-7951b8182714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61dac524-823b-5e13-b56d-133c28aec53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360047} :answer-field/id #uuid "458fa144-24b7-5a14-a379-5e62a18ed85d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0fa48839-143d-5a0b-8b2f-09125573ccac" :question/math-academy-id "q-79876" :question/problem "Estimate $(1.06)^{4.1}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{y}$ at the point $(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =4\\cdot {1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{1}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈4\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n(1.06)^{4.1}=f(1.06,4.1),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1.06,4.1).$\n\nMoving from $(1,4)$ to $(1.06,4.1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.06-1=0.06, \\\\ Δy & =4.1-4=0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈4\\cdot (0.06)+0\\cdot (0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.24+0 \\\\  & ≈0.24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.24.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.06,4.1) & ≈f(1,4)+0.24 \\\\ (1.06)^{4.1} & ≈{1}^{4}+0.24 \\\\  & ≈1+0.24 \\\\  & ≈1.24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee984fef-dfa3-5c07-a7ad-eb2e15b08360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360051} {:answer/id #uuid "116262b2-e5ad-5d71-b1fb-0ee218f844a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360052} {:answer/id #uuid "a520640a-07a2-5431-bad2-906fbbbed2db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360053} {:answer/id #uuid "413db8fc-c1b2-5ae3-8842-885ebb0c236f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360054} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f8826f-291c-500b-9535-2a5b7a218e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3f8826f-291c-500b-9535-2a5b7a218e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360055} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff4a3a4-2864-5eb3-bd8e-28f01e3e2df7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cd05d8b-6c57-5623-803a-2f4730ea02a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-86715" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{3}-y),$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(2,-3,1)$ to $(1,-2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2x\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{4}{{z}^{3}-y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{12{z}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-y})\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,-3,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,-3,1) & =(2\\cdot 2)\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{4}{{1}^{3}-(-3)})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{12\\cdot {1}^{2}}{{1}^{3}-(-3)})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{d}x-\\,\\text{d}y+3\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,-3+Δy,1+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈4\\,Δx-\\,Δy+3\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,-3,1)$ to $(1,-2,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-2=-1, \\\\ Δy & =-2-(-3)=1 \\\\ Δz & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈4\\,Δx-1\\,Δy+3\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈4\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ≈-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ decreases by approximately $-2.$"}]}
